  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 139 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nThe aforementioned upper low will begin to push eastward by early \nTuesday and move over the Desert Southwest through the day Tuesday. \nThe overall potential for heavy rainfall late Tuesday into Wednesday \nis still there, but there still is much to be desired from model \nconsensus. Many models, including their ensembles, still bring heavy \nrainfall into the FA by late Tuesday beginning around sunset. The \nGFS and GDPS are the most aggressive with QPF. The ECMWF has come to \nbe the most pessimistic, at least as of the 00Z run. It is roughly 6 \nhours slower than the GFS with the travel speed of the upper low, \nand pulls the upper low further north as it crosses New Mexico. The \npath of the upper low will have a significant effect on the \npotential for heavy rainfall, but this will take another day or two \nto determine which path is more likely. The forecast will continue \nto side with wetter guidance given the larger consensus. \nAdditionally, moisture will be very abundant given the influx of \nboth moisture from the Gulf and from the tropical systems in the \neastern Pacific. This will supply PWATs as high as 2+ inches, an \nanomaly of at least +1 inches, and allow for efficient rain \nproduction. We will continue to hold of on the issuance of a Flood \nWatch until confidence grows. The upper low will stall over the \nSouthern Plains by late next week as an upper high builds over the \nsoutheastern CONUS. This should allow for additional light rain \nshowers across the region. Models diverge greatly with the upper \npattern next weekend and beyond. The GFS builds a large ridge over \nthe western CONUS while the ECMWF has more zonal-like flow.",
